WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:01.990
(dramatic music)

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:04.610
- [Narrator] Americans are traveling again

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.240
but if your summer travel
plans involve a rental car,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.490
be prepared for long
lines and sticker shock.

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.690
- I think it's all over the country.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:16.790
There isn't one area where you
can find a cheap rental car.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.430
- Using a car right now is expensive,

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.210
whether you're buying it or renting it.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.520
- [Narrator] According to
the travel website Kayak,

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.140
rental car prices in the
first two weeks of June

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.570
are up 89% for the July 4th weekend,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.750
compared with the same dates in 2019

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.000
and as much as 300% in some places.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.490
But even if you decide to
pony up the additional cash

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:40.640
for a vehicle,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.030
many reservations are
simply not being honored

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.520
by rental agencies

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.160
and travelers have reported
showing up at rental counters,

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.600
only to be turned away

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.800
because there aren't
enough cars on the lot.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.500
So why have rental cars
become so expensive?

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.200
And how long can we expect
these high prices to last?

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.840
- It's a pretty classic
supply and demand issue.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.970
There are not a lot of
rental cars to go around

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.270
because these companies
downsized their fleets

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.920
when nobody was using them.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.340
At the beginning of the pandemic,

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.970
travel of any kind really collapsed,

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.300
but particularly air travel,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.340
which the rental car companies
rely on very heavily.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.530
And so there was this situation

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.250
where they had to react very quickly

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.250
to a very rapid drop in demand.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.030
- [Narrator] For months, the
pandemic transformed airports

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.780
into ghost towns, which in turn wiped out

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.370
about 2/3 of the rental car business.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.970
- What happens is all of
the rental car companies

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.030
react quickly, sell off their cars

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.540
before they lose too much value.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.010
Downsize their fleets, cut
costs, downsize their staff.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.690
I mean, find every way to pinch pennies

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:55.330
and stay alive really.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.550
It was a make-or-break
moment for these companies.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.613
- [Narrator] To survive,
many rental car agencies

00:02:00.613 --> 00:02:02.870
sold off large parts of their fleet.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:04.510
- It was a great time to sell cars too

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:08.810
because new car production
still hasn't normalized.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:10.800
So they sold a lot of cars

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.550
to build cash flow

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.850
but then they also sold cars

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.110
in order to right size to demand.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:19.410
And so you had a double whammy

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.470
in terms of supply getting cut.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.021
And then you also had Hertz go bankrupt.

00:02:24.021 --> 00:02:26.640
(pensive music)

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.970
- [Narrator] In May of 2020,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.160
Hertz filed for bankruptcy

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.980
but Mazari sees a silver
lining in the disruption.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:35.540
- COVID was a blessing in
disguise for this industry

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.310
because it created pricing discipline

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:39.910
and supply discipline.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.360
These companies basically had
to cut their fleet size down

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.360
by 35%, so this was a
dysfunctional oligopoly.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.540
Supply was never tight.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.940
- [Narrator] This summer,
consumer demand has returned

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.460
and now a new problem has arisen.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:58.380
There's an extreme shortage of new cars.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:02:59.470
- When demand came back,

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.690
it did not trickle back.

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:02.750
It came back almost overnight

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:03.880
and you have these companies

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.740
that have right sized themselves

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:10.740
to a market that basically
wasn't using them before.

00:03:10.905 --> 00:03:13.650
And they just weren't prepared.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.120
- We're also hearing
is that these companies

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.930
are so desperate to get fleet

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.900
that they're buying cars
in the used car market,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.590
which is in the auction market.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.350
So don't be surprised if
you get into some rental car

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.033
that has a lot more miles on it.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.230
- [Narrator] Exacerbating
the car shortage issue

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.210
is another shortage: semiconductors.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:36.730
Automakers have said the production

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:40.220
of new vehicles has been slowed
because of chip shortages.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.330
- New car production with a chip shortage

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.760
isn't gonna normalize until Q4.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.580
So for these companies,
it's gonna be very tough

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.860
to add fleet back this year.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.390
And so this year, pricing's
gonna remain very high.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:53.410
- [Narrator] In separate statements

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:54.650
to The Wall Street Journal,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.710
both Hertz and Enterprise acknowledged

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:00.060
that customers might have a
tougher time securing a rental

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.180
because of the spike in demand.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:03.187
Enterprise wrote,

00:04:03.187 --> 00:04:05.970
"While still below
pre-pandemic fleet levels,

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.670
our fleet is not down significantly.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.260
A key challenge right now

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.930
is the global chip shortage,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.590
impacting new car availability."

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.750
Hertz wrote, "The microchip shortage

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.410
is also affecting the car rental industry

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:20.680
but Hertz is working closely

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.100
with our automotive partners

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.090
to add new vehicles to our fleet

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.900
as quickly as possible
while also moving vehicles

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.188
to the areas with highest demand."

00:04:29.188 --> 00:04:31.000
(dramatic music)

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.010
If you have to rent a car this summer,

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:36.650
Mazari recommends booking
early and booking backwards.

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:40.040
Typically, travelers book
flights or hotels first

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.020
but that strategy won't work this year.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.890
- You essentially, you just
have to book in advance

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.667
and kind of pay up for it

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:50.729
and be prepared for that, if
you're lucky enough to get one.

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:53.479
(dramatic music)

